# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit .env — .gitignore already excludes it.

# ── Application ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
APP_PORT=3001

# ── PostgreSQL ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
POSTGRES_DB=olibra
POSTGRES_USER=olibra

# Required. compose.yaml guards this with `${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:?set
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD in .env}`, but that guard only rejects a genuinely empty
# or unset value — and an inline comment used to sit at the end of this very
# line, which is neither. Compose keeps everything after `=` in a `.env`
# file, comment included, so `POSTGRES_PASSWORD=          # required, no
# default — compose refuses to start without it` set the superuser's actual
# password to that sentence, spaces and all. Verified live: `docker inspect
# olibra-db-1` showed exactly that string as `POSTGRES_PASSWORD`, and
# Postgres had already started and accepted it — the sentence's own claim was
# false in the one case it mattered. `MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL` below cannot
# then be written by hand, because nobody can type a password they were never
# told. Leave this line with nothing after `=` and compose really will
# refuse to start; either way, fill in a real value before running it.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_PORT=5435

# CRITICAL 2. Password for `olibra_pool`, the connection-pool login role
# 20260808_13_pool_role.sql creates: granted membership in `olibra_app` (and,
# only for the deliberate cross-shelf admin path, `olibra_admin`), and
# deliberately NOT a superuser and NOT bypassrls — both Postgres defaults for
# a freshly created role, left untouched. Every RLS guarantee in this project
# is inert on a superuser connection regardless of which role it `set local
# role`s to afterwards (DATABASE.md §3), which is exactly what the
# application's connection used to be.
#
# Required, no default, the same as POSTGRES_PASSWORD above — and the same
# reason this comment lives on its own line rather than trailing the
# variable: compose.yaml's `${OLIBRA_POOL_PASSWORD:?...}` guard only rejects
# a genuinely empty value, and an inline comment here used to make this
# line's value that comment's own text instead. Keep it in sync by hand with
# DATABASE_URL and TEST_POOL_DATABASE_URL below: dotenv does not expand
# `${VAR}` references inside this file, the same reason DATABASE_URL already
# repeats POSTGRES_PASSWORD's value rather than referencing it.
OLIBRA_POOL_PASSWORD=

# What the running application connects as — olibra_pool, not the olibra
# superuser above. Inside compose the host is `db`; running the app outside
# compose against the same database, it is localhost. Write the password by
# hand (dotenv does not expand `${VAR}`), never by pasting in whatever
# `docker inspect` reports for a variable that swallowed a comment — src/
# instrumentation.ts refuses to start if this URL's password looks like it
# did.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://olibra_pool:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5435/olibra

# What `bun run db:migrate` and `bun run db:seed` connect as — deliberately
# still the olibra superuser, not olibra_pool: creating roles, enabling row
# level security and `force row level security`, and 0010_rls.sql's own
# `create role` statements all need privileges olibra_pool must never have.
# Never read by the running application itself — see src/db/migrate-cli.ts
# and src/db/seed-cli.ts, the only two callers. Same guard as DATABASE_URL
# above against a password that is actually a swallowed comment.
MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgres://olibra:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5435/olibra

# ── Object storage (S3-compatible) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# The application speaks S3 and nothing else. MinIO is what runs in compose,
# but these variables are deliberately provider-neutral: pointing them at AWS
# S3, Cloudflare R2 or Backblaze B2 is the whole of the migration.
#
#   MinIO (compose)   S3_ENDPOINT=http://storage:9000   S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
#   AWS S3            S3_ENDPOINT=                      S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=false
#   Cloudflare R2     S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#                                                       S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
S3_REGION=us-east-1
S3_BUCKET=olibra

# Required — compose.yaml's `${S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID:?...}` becomes MinIO's root
# user. Moved off this line for the same reason POSTGRES_PASSWORD's comment
# was: an inline comment here is a value, not a note, to whatever reads this
# file.
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=

# Required, at least 8 characters for MinIO. Same reason as above.
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true

# Where a browser fetches an object from. Differs from S3_ENDPOINT whenever the
# app reaches storage over the internal network but the browser does not.
S3_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:9000

S3_PORT=9000
S3_CONSOLE_PORT=9001

# ── Locale ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Fixed by §4 of the requirements regardless of where the process runs.
TZ=Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh

# ── Tests ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The test database is a separate compose service on its own port, behind the
# `test` profile so an ordinary `up` does not start it:
#
#   docker compose --profile test up -d db-test
#
# It is never the development database: the suite truncates every table, and
# tests/support/env.ts refuses any URL that does not name olibra_test.
#
# NOTE: the port below is duplicated, not derived — POSTGRES_TEST_PORT feeds
# db-test's compose port mapping, TEST_DATABASE_URL's port is a separate
# literal read directly by the app/test process. Changing one without the
# other silently points the suite at a closed port instead of db-test.
POSTGRES_TEST_PORT=5436
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://olibra:olibra@localhost:5436/olibra_test

# CRITICAL 2's acceptance test (tests/auth/pool-role.test.ts) needs a second
# handle to db-test that is provably not a superuser, to run the guard path
# (signIn, contextFor, ...) through the same non-superuser role the real
# application uses — tests/support/db.ts's shared `sql` handle cannot become
# that connection itself, since it also does setup and truncation that role
# has no privilege to do. Same password as OLIBRA_POOL_PASSWORD above:
# migrate() creates olibra_pool with whichever value that variable holds,
# regardless of which database (dev or test) it is applied to, so this URL's
# password must match it exactly.
TEST_POOL_DATABASE_URL=postgres://olibra_pool:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5436/olibra_test

# The storage suite (tests/storage/s3.test.ts) runs against a real MinIO, the
# same way the database suite runs against a real PostgreSQL — it puts an
# object and then `fetch`es the URL the store hands out, which is the only way
# to check "a browser can fetch what the server wrote" rather than restate it.
# So the compose storage service has to be running:
#
#   docker compose up -d storage
#
# It is never the development bucket: the suite deletes objects, and
# tests/support/env.ts refuses any TEST_S3_BUCKET that does not carry `test` as
# a delimited word (so `olibra-test` yes, `prod-testimonials` no). The bucket
# itself needs no setup — the suite creates it.
#
# TEST_S3_ENDPOINT is guarded too, and more sharply, because the suite does not
# only delete out of that bucket: it applies a policy making it readable by
# anyone on the internet. A mistyped endpoint would therefore publish somebody
# else's bucket, which no backup undoes. So it has to be loopback — localhost,
# 127.0.0.1 or ::1. If you genuinely need a throwaway object store elsewhere,
# name its host again in the line below; repeating the host rather than setting
# a boolean means the next typo is still refused. Left commented on purpose:
# uncommented, tests/architecture/ci-supplies-required-env.test.ts would require
# it in the CI workflow, and CI's MinIO is on localhost like everyone else's.
#
#   TEST_S3_ALLOW_REMOTE_ENDPOINT=storage.example.org
#
# These mirror the seven S3_* variables above rather than reusing them, for the
# same reason TEST_DATABASE_URL is separate from DATABASE_URL: pointing the
# suite somewhere must never touch what the application reads. The credentials
# below are MinIO's root user, so they match S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID /
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY above — duplicated by hand, since dotenv does not expand
# `${VAR}` references inside this file.
TEST_S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
TEST_S3_REGION=us-east-1
TEST_S3_BUCKET=olibra-test

# Required, same as S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID above — moved off this line for the same
# reason POSTGRES_PASSWORD's comment was.
TEST_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=

# Required, same as S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY above.
TEST_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
TEST_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
TEST_S3_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:9000
